      "content": "#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <climits>\n\nint main() {\n\tint n, s;\n\tstd::cin >> n >> s;\n\tstd::vector<int> matrix(n * n);\n\tfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j)\n\t\t\tscanf(\"%d\", &matrix[i * n + j]);\n\n\t// Column sums of the first S rows\n\tstd::vector<long long> colSum(n);\n\t#pragma omp simd\n\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) colSum[j] = matrix[0 * n + j];\n\tfor (int i = 1; i < s; ++i) {\n\t\tconst int base = i * n;\n\t\t#pragma omp simd\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) colSum[j] += matrix[base + j];\n\t}\n\n\tlong long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n\n\t// Slide the vertical window down one row at a time\n\tfor (int top = 0; top + s <= n; ++top) {\n\t\t// Horizontal sliding window of length S over colSum\n\t\tlong long window = 0;\n\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) window += colSum[j];\n\t\tlong long best = window;\n\t\tfor (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\twindow += colSum[j] - colSum[j - s];\n\t\t\tif (window > best) best = window;\n\t\t}\n\t\tif (best > mxSum) mxSum = best;\n\n\t\t// Move the vertical window down by one row\n\t\tif (top + s < n) {\n\t\t\tconst int addBase = (top + s) * n;\n\t\t\tconst int subBase = top * n;\n\t\t\t#pragma omp simd\n\t\t\tfor (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n\t\t\t\tcolSum[j] += matrix[addBase + j] - matrix[subBase + j];\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tprintf(\"%lld\\n\", mxSum);\n\treturn 0;\n}",
      "path": "src/solution.cpp"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Flattening the NxN matrix from a vector-of-vectors into a single contiguous row-major std::vector<int> improves cache locality for the column-sum initialization and vertical-window update loops. The flat layout enables more efficient SIMD loads/stores (the #pragma omp simd loops now access strictly consecutive addresses) while preserving all loop-carried dependencies: the horizontal sliding-window recurrence remains scalar, and each colSum element is updated independently across j.",
  "notes": "The transformation only changes memory representation and index arithmetic; no algorithmic or dependency structure is altered. Base indices are hoisted out of inner loops to avoid repeated multiplication. All valid sizes, seeds, and repeated calls remain correct because the flat indexing exactly mirrors row-major access of the original nested vectors.",
